  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1000 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nAnother copy and paste evening currently progresses across the\nMid-South with temperatures in the 50s to 60s. Clear skies and\ncalm winds will give way to decent cooling once again into early\nFriday morning, with lows falling into the 40s to 50s. Very\nlittle changes to the overall forecast are expected for Friday,\nwith an upper-level ridge continuing to build through the region.\nAn unseasonably warm and dry air mass will remain, similar to the\npast few days, with high temperatures reaching the 70s to low 80s\nand dew point temperatures falling to the upper 30s to 40s. This\nwill spell continued elevated fire danger concerns, so please\ncontinue to heed to any local burn bans and restrictions.\n\nInto the upcoming weekend and next week, there are very little\nchanges to the overall forecast as a dry weather pattern persists\nthrough at least the middle of next week. The aforementioned\nridge will remain parked over the region Saturday, before\neventually beginning to break down into next week. Over this\ntimeframe, high temperatures will generally remain about 5 to\n10 degrees above normal for the end of October. While we may\nstill see a few locations reach into the low 80s on Saturday,\nSunday onward we will likely see highs capped in the 70s as a\nslightly cooler air mass filters in. Overnight lows will remain\nin the 40s to low 50s through this period.\n\nBy the middle to end of next week, we may finally begin to see a\npattern shift. There are still notable differences in LREF\nguidance with the timing and strength of a low pressure system\nout of the northwest U.S. Some guidance does bring a pattern\nshift back to more seasonable temperatures with precipitation\nfinally returning, while others keep a dry and slightly warm\nforecast. As such, there is little confidence in the forecast\nnear the end of the current forecast period, but there could\nfinally be a potential end in sight to the copy / paste forecast\nwe have seen recently.",
